# Virtual Christian — Quiet-by-Design at MWAA: the wrong problem, framed the wrong way

**Role in this run:** The "wait, have you thought about it this way?" voice. Independent brief. I have not read the other agents' outputs.

---

## The reframe

**Quiet is not an acoustic specification. Quiet is a lagging indicator of trust — trust that the visual system is right, the app is right, the staff will find you, and the airline will not surprise you.** Every airport that got to "quiet" got there because passengers stopped asking to be told. If passengers stop trusting the alternatives, they demand audio — and the airline, the gate agent, and TSA will give it to them, whether the design manual says they can or not. Specifying quiet without simultaneously specifying the redundant-information stack that must be operational and staffed during irregular operations is the airport equivalent of a hospital declaring "quiet hours" without buying pagers. It works on a Tuesday afternoon. It falls apart the first snowstorm.

That is the whole argument. Everything below supports it.

---

## Five connect-the-dots arguments

### 1. The hospital alarm-fatigue playbook is the right template, and it says: route the message, don't quiet the room.

Hospitals fought this exact battle a decade ago and lost most of the first round. A nurse in an ICU hears an average of 1.2 alarms per minute; a single medical procedure can generate 359 of them. Most people cannot reliably distinguish more than six alarm sounds. The result was "alarm fatigue" — the clinically documented condition in which staff stop hearing the alarms that matter because they are drowning in the ones that don't. The fix was not to make the hospital quieter. It was to route each alarm to the individual clinician who needed it, on their own device, with smart escalation.

**That is the correct model for an airport PA.** The mistake in most "quiet airport" programs is to treat announcements as an environmental variable to be dialed down. They are an information-routing problem. If MWAA specifies a PA architecture that can address a single gate, a single hold room, or a single passenger's phone by geofence, then "quiet" becomes an emergent property of the routing choice rather than a policy fought over with every gate agent every day. Copy the hospital. Do not copy the "shush" sign in the library.

### 2. Every quiet peer airport is quiet because the operator can enforce silence. MWAA is a landlord, not a sovereign.

Helsinki-Vantaa's "Silent Airport" project — the origin case that half the industry cites — was run jointly by Finavia and the airlines operating there, and it exempted gate areas and emergency announcements from day one. That model works because Finavia is a state-owned airport company that can, in effect, negotiate directly with a national flag carrier that dominates its traffic. Schiphol, Copenhagen, London City, Dubai — same basic structure. State or single-operator, dominant home carrier, contract regime European in flavor.

MWAA is a public-body corporate under a US signatory-carrier Use and Lease Agreement. The new 15-year U&L that took effect January 1, 2025 binds airlines to rates and charges, not to a PA policy. If MWAA writes "no non-emergency announcements in the concourse" into its Design Manual, but United's gate agent at IAD grabs the microphone during a Chicago ground stop because that is what she has always done — what happens? Nothing. There is no enforcement mechanism unless the U&L or a tenant-technology exhibit says there is. **The peer-benchmark literature is describing an institutional relationship MWAA does not have.** The right question is not "how quiet should the terminal be?" but "what contract instrument will make it stay that way when the airline decides otherwise at 6 PM on a Friday in December?"

### 3. The $2.8 million lesson from another airport terminal: acoustics is not a spec you write at the end.

There is a documented case, cited in the STIPA measurement literature, in which an unnamed airport terminal's acoustic consultant predicted a Speech Transmission Index of 0.38 — well below the NFPA-72 required 0.50 for emergency voice systems in required areas. The prediction was "documented but overruled by architectural vision." The measured STI came in at 0.32–0.42. The airport spent $2.8 million and eight weeks of disruption to fix it after the fact.

That is the airport version of the restaurant-acoustics problem: modern dining rooms with glass, tile, and exposed structure often measure reverberation times (RT60) of 1.5 to 3.0 seconds, roughly three times the 0.6–1.0-second target that lets a person hold a conversation across a table. **The same aesthetic that makes airport terminals photogenic — glass curtain walls, terrazzo floors, exposed steel, twenty-meter ceilings — is precisely the aesthetic that destroys speech intelligibility.** Concourse E at IAD is roughly 435,000 square feet, opening this autumn. Terminal 2 at DCA is Cesar Pelli vaulted volumes. Whatever quiet-by-design standard MWAA writes has to be enforceable at the massing stage — not after the renderings are approved and the architect is defending the atrium. If the standard sits in the acoustic section of a technical exhibit, it will lose to the section that has the photograph.

### 4. NHTSA had to require EVs to make noise. Quiet has failure modes.

When electric vehicles arrived, they were too quiet — pedestrians, and especially visually impaired pedestrians, could not hear them coming. NHTSA, under the Pedestrian Safety Enhancement Act, now requires all hybrid and electric vehicles under 10,000 pounds to emit an acoustic vehicle alerting sound in the 43–64 dB band at speeds up to 18.6 mph and in reverse. The agency estimated the mandate would prevent about 2,400 pedestrian injuries per year once fleet compliance was full.

**Read that as an aviation parable.** Every quiet system has to be engineered with its failure mode in mind. In airports, the analog is: what happens to a blind traveler in a "quiet zone" hold room when the visual FIDS is the only channel telling them the gate has changed? What happens to a non-English-speaking connecting international passenger at IAD — one of the 10.5 million international passengers Dulles saw in CY 2025 — when the airport has removed the boarding-call PA on the theory that "everyone has an app"? The right standard is not "less audio." It is engineered audio — targeted, redundant, and specified against a defined set of failure modes for defined user groups. Otherwise "quiet-by-design" is a subsidy to travelers who look like the designers, paid for by everyone who does not.

### 5. Modern libraries stopped trying to be quiet everywhere and started zoning. Airports should follow.

Public libraries went through this reckoning fifteen years ago. The "shush" model excluded working families, teenagers, immigrants using computers, and anyone whose life did not fit the middle-class norm of silent reading. The response was zoning: silent study rooms, quiet reading zones, collaboration areas, kids' zones, maker spaces — often within the same building, sometimes on the same floor. The library got quieter *and* more used.

Pittsburgh International Airport's Presley's Place, opened in 2019, is the aviation-industry proof that this works: a 1,500-square-foot sensory suite with a soundproofed transitional entrance, individual and family rooms, and a mock aircraft cabin, all designed for travelers with autism and other sensory sensitivities. It is now the reference design for airport sensory rooms globally. **The lesson is not "build one sensory room." The lesson is that different passengers need different soundscapes at different points in the journey, and the mature answer is zoning, not a uniform decibel target.** IAD's curb-to-gate journey and DCA's are not the same journey, and the passenger arriving on the AeroTrain from a nine-hour transatlantic flight is not in the same acoustic state as the DCA short-haul business traveler at 6 AM. A single system standard that ignores this is neither humane nor operationally correct.

