## The operator's gut check

*The line I would actually say at the bar, word for word:*

**"You want a quieter airport? Fix the reasons the gate agent picks up the microphone. Quiet is what happens when your data is right, your app is on time, and you have enough humans on the floor. It is not what happens when your consultant writes STI 0.50 into an exhibit and calls it a day."**

Operator translation for the sophisticated reader: I have stood on an ops floor during an IROPS event. The reason airports get loud is not that we forgot to specify quiet. It is that when the FAA drops a ground stop at 4 PM and the gate agent has 180 people staring at her and the app push has not fired yet and the FIDS still says "on time" and there is nobody at the customer-service desk because the vendor is short-staffed — she is going to grab the microphone. She should grab the microphone. Any standard that pretends otherwise will be violated on its first snowstorm and then quietly ignored forever after, which is the worst of both worlds: you paid the design premium and you got neither the quiet nor the compliance.

The MWAA-specific version: DCA is still absorbing the safety and political aftershocks of the January 29, 2025 midair collision. Any recommendation that reads as "we are turning down the safety-communication volume" — even if that is not what it says — is going to have a very hard time surviving the board room and a harder time surviving the first NTSB question after the next incident. The standard needs to lead with "louder and more targeted when it matters, silent when it does not," not with "quiet."

