## Productive tangents

I followed a few side threads. Two paid off, two did not. I kept the ones that did not because the negative result matters.

### Paid off

- **Path dependency of the airport PA.** The reason terminals are loud in the first place traces to railroad and 1950s carrier-terminal PA conventions that we imported without ever revisiting the assumption. This matters because it means the correct default is "no announcement unless required," inverted from today's "announcement unless prohibited." Writing that inversion into the Design Manual is a one-sentence change with enormous downstream effect.
- **The FIDS-and-app trust budget.** If a passenger sees the FIDS say "on time" while the app says "delayed 45 minutes," you have burned trust that no amount of acoustic treatment can restore. Quiet-by-design has to include an information-consistency standard across FIDS, app, gate screens, and PA. That is a systems-integration standard, not an acoustic one, and it belongs in the tenant-technology exhibit of the U&L, not in the acoustic section of the Design Manual.

### Did not pay off, but worth noting

- **Casinos and designed soundscape.** I chased the casino-audio analogy — casinos engineer sound rather than remove it. Interesting, but the incentive structure is inverted: casinos want you to stay and lose track of time; airports want you to move to your gate and be findable. The analogy does not carry the argument, but it did clarify why "designed silence" is a better target than "designed sound" for aviation.
- **Highway variable-message-sign fatigue.** DOTs learned that VMS displaying non-urgent content trains drivers to ignore urgent content. There is an obvious parallel to FIDS and PA. I could not find a clean citation to a US FHWA study inside a reasonable search window, so I am leaving this as a directional analogy rather than an evidenced claim. Worth someone else's follow-up.

